WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.075 --> 00:00:02.712
- [Narrator] Silly
string, that bright, fun,

00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:06.365
party in an aerosol can, was
actually invented by mistake.

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:07.954
Believe it or not, it was supposed to

00:00:07.954 --> 00:00:10.287
help heal your broken bones.

00:00:12.398 --> 00:00:14.867
In 1972, Leonard A. Fish, an inventor,

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:16.492
and Robert P. Cox, a chemist, were granted

00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.767
a U.S. patent for a foamable
resinous composition.

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:22.188
The two initially set out
to create an aerosol can

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.070
that could spray an instant
cast on a broken leg or arm,

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.868
and the invention worked,

00:00:26.868 --> 00:00:29.014
but when it came to
packaging their discovery,

00:00:29.014 --> 00:00:31.652
they tested 500 different
types of nozzles.

00:00:31.652 --> 00:00:33.234
Through their tests, Fish came upon one

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:35.226
that produced a string, a string

00:00:35.226 --> 00:00:38.100
that shot 30 feet across the room.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.188
Inspired, they decided to turn

00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:41.242
their instant cast into a toy.

00:00:41.242 --> 00:00:43.558
After altering their formula to produce

00:00:43.558 --> 00:00:45.638
a more colorful, less sticky string,

00:00:45.638 --> 00:00:48.002
the pair set out to market their product.

00:00:48.002 --> 00:00:49.656
Neither of them knew the first
thing about selling toys,

00:00:49.656 --> 00:00:52.577
so they made an appointment
with Wham-O in California.

00:00:52.577 --> 00:00:55.543
But, the meeting didn't
go exactly as planned.

00:00:55.543 --> 00:00:58.418
Fish and Cox sprayed the
string all over the office,

00:00:58.418 --> 00:01:00.266
including on the person
they were meeting with,

00:01:00.266 --> 00:01:01.680
and unsurprisingly--

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:02.513
- [Woman] Get out!

00:01:02.513 --> 00:01:04.502
- They were asked to leave the premises.

00:01:04.502 --> 00:01:06.662
Just one day later, Fish
receives a telegram from Wham-O

00:01:06.662 --> 00:01:09.557
asking him to send 24
cans for a market test.

00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:11.665
Turns out that a trace of the silly string

00:01:11.665 --> 00:01:13.304
was left on a lamp shade in the office

00:01:13.304 --> 00:01:15.214
and was later spotted by Wham-O's owners,

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:18.556
who themselves demanded a
market test immediately.

00:01:18.556 --> 00:01:21.518
Two weeks later, Wham-O signed a contract

00:01:21.518 --> 00:01:26.133
to license the product
now known as silly string.

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.966
(whimsical music)

